    "narrative": "Our strongest plays today are headlined by **Josh Naylor** (75.57) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92), both of whom capitalize on high-upside matchups against pitchers with xERAs above 5.00. **Vinnie Pasquantino** is particularly well-positioned, benefiting from a significant 14% boost due to dynamic platoon advantages, while **Josh Naylor** finds his floor raised by a +19% adjustment for facing Imai. **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.71) also warrants attention; while he faces a mid-tier arm in Pallante, his score is bolstered by a +10% park factor, cementing his status as a must-start alongside the speed-threat **James Wood** (68.73), whose spot at the top of the lineup remains a vital catalyst for our stolen base production.\n\nThe decision to bench **Trevor Story** (46.48) was the most difficult call of the morning. While he offered a decent base projection, the matchup against an elite arm like Wheeler proved prohibitive, dragging down his efficiency score once accounting for the negative impacts of an elite opposing catcher and defensive hold. **Trevor Story** ultimately failed to clear the threshold required to displace the high-efficiency slots currently held by **Brice Turang** and **Cole Young**. We are keeping a close watch on **Mickey Moniak** (54.25); despite a strong 14% platoon edge, his matchup against Skenes presents a major volatility risk. Our bench remains depleted by the absences of **Jose Caballero** and **Giancarlo Stanton**, so we will proceed with the current configuration, noting that any further injuries or defensive adjustments will require us to leverage the depth of **Zach McKinstry** or the broader organization if the current floor begins to erode.",
